{"records":[{"id":"j3w36zb6vy","title":"Supplemental Table S1. Full clinicopathologic characteristics of IVLBCL and DLBCL cases with cutaneous recurrence","description":"Supplemental Table S1 containing full clinicopathologic characteristics of intravascular large B-cell lymphoma (IVLBCL) and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) cases with cutaneous recurrence. This file accompanies the associated JAAD Case Reports manuscript \"Systemic intravascular large B-cell lymphoma relapsing as an extravascular cutaneous large B-cell lymphoma after eight years from initial diagnosis.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-18T14:38:28.658Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j3w36zb6vy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j3w36zb6vy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Seongbae","last_name":"Kong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joseph","last_name":"Durgin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tera","last_name":"Mayer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dahlia","last_name":"Sano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexandra","last_name":"Hristov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Trilokraj","last_name":"Tejasvi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma of the Skin","Immunohistochemistry","B-Cell Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dby7kdtzk8","title":"Cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-Positive Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma, not otherwise specified: A systematic review and comparative analysis with Epstein-Barr Virus-negative, leg type","description":"Supplement 1. PRISMA flow diagram of literature search and study selection. \nEBV+ DLBCL, NOS, Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified; PRISMA, Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses.\n\nSupplement 2. Histopathological features of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified. (A) Pleomorphic atypical large cells in a background rich in reactive cells such as lymphohistiocytes and plasma cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). (B) Monotonous sheets of immunoblast-like large cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). Atypical large lymphoid cells of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified highlighted by (C) MUM-1 (x200), (D) BCL-2 (x200), and (E) CD30 (x200); Epstein-Barr Virus infection detected by (F) positive Epstein-Barr encoding region in situ hybridization (x200).\n\nSupplement 3. Survival analysis of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified (EBV+ DLBCL, NOS). (A) Difference in overall survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and primary cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-negative diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, leg type (EBV- DLBCL, leg type). (B) Comparison in survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and concurrent cutaneous and extracutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-27T07:21:33.083Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dby7kdtzk8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dby7kdtzk8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Woo Jin ","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma","Lymphoma of the Skin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f4kndmp8c5","title":"MicroRNAs as prognostic biomarkers in patients with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","description":"MicroRNAs as prognostic biomarkers in patients with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma，Relevant raw data and codes, etc.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-24T18:01:35.606Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f4kndmp8c5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f4kndmp8c5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yunfei","last_name":"zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["microRNA","Exosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rstwmcw94x","title":"Mass Cytometry of Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (CyTOF cohort)","description":"This dataset contains mass cytometry data from 41 diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLB) and 31 reactive lymph node (RLN) samples. Each sample was stained with a 40-antibody B cell panel and a 39-antibody T cell panel and acquired on a CyTOF2 instrument. The samples were acquired across 5 separate batch runs (#2-6). Each batch run also included an aliquot from a pool of 13 different RLN samples to serve as an internal staining/acquisition batch control. The file naming format is \"CytB2_DLB_A1234\" where \"Cyt\" = CyTOF, \"B\" = B cell panel (or \"T\" = T cell panel), \"2\" = batch #2 (or 3, 4, 5, or 6), \"DLB\" = diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (or \"RLN\" = reactive lymph node), and \"A1234\" = unique sample identifier.\n\nDiffuse large B-cell lymphoma is a common malignancy of mature B cells with heterogeneous outcomes. Prior studies have defined poor prognosis subtypes by features such as cell-of-origin; however, the role of the immune microenvironment in defining clinical outcomes is less clear. Here we used highly dimensional mass and flow cytometry with up to 40 and 22 antibody markers, respectively, to derive a single cell-resolved map of the tumor ecosystem encompassing both B- and T-cell compartments, along with reactive lymph node controls. Unlike our recent findings in follicular lymphoma (FL), B-cell phenotypes were largely unique to each patient and intratumoral diversity was neither prominent nor correlated with clinical outcome. Among tumor infiltrating T-cells, multidimensional analysis yielded 23-25 distinct clusters representing the full spectrum of activation/maturation states including terminally differentiated subsets which identified patients with inferior clinical outcome amongst a discovery cohort of 123 patients. Importantly, this observation was validated in an independent cohort of 151 patients using a single marker of terminal differentiation, CD57, in combination with routine CD4/CD8 T cell subsetting markers thus simplifying translation to routine clinical practice. The significance of CD8+ CD57+ T-cells was independent of cell-of-origin classification, suggesting this immune response feature is relevant across DLBCL subtypes. T-cell profiling can thus provide insight into potential tumor/immune cell interactions and may inform selection of immune-directed therapies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-17T14:21:27.725Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rstwmcw94x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rstwmcw94x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Weng","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03gqhbs95","name":"BC Cancer Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/04m0xav37","name":"Leukemia and Lymphoma Society","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cancer","B-Cell Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vndw5rx78b","title":"Flow Cytometry of Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (Fortessa cohort)","description":"This dataset contains 13- or 14-color flow cytometry data from 151 diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLB) diagnostic biopsy samples. Each sample was stained with a 13- or 14-antibody T cell panel and data were acquired on a BD LSR Fortessa instrument. The file naming format is \"ForT_DLB_A1234\" where \"For\" = Fortessa, \"T\" = T cell panel, \"DLB\" = diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, and \"A1234\" = unique sample identifier.\n\nDiffuse large B-cell lymphoma is a common malignancy of mature B cells with heterogeneous outcomes. Prior studies have defined poor prognosis subtypes by features such as cell-of-origin; however, the role of the immune microenvironment in defining clinical outcomes is less clear. Here we used highly dimensional mass and flow cytometry with up to 40 and 22 antibody markers, respectively, to derive a single cell-resolved map of the tumor ecosystem encompassing both B- and T-cell compartments, along with reactive lymph node controls. Unlike our recent findings in follicular lymphoma (FL), B-cell phenotypes were largely unique to each patient and intratumoral diversity was neither prominent nor correlated with clinical outcome. Among tumor infiltrating T-cells, multidimensional analysis yielded 23-25 distinct clusters representing the full spectrum of activation/maturation states including terminally differentiated subsets which identified patients with inferior clinical outcome amongst a discovery cohort of 123 patients. Importantly, this observation was validated in an independent cohort of 151 patients using a single marker of terminal differentiation, CD57, in combination with routine CD4/CD8 T cell subsetting markers thus simplifying translation to routine clinical practice. The significance of CD8+ CD57+ T-cells was independent of cell-of-origin classification, suggesting this immune response feature is relevant across DLBCL subtypes. T-cell profiling can thus provide insight into potential tumor/immune cell interactions and may inform selection of immune-directed therapies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-17T14:22:22.651Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vndw5rx78b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vndw5rx78b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Weng","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04m0xav37","name":"Leukemia and Lymphoma Society","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/03gqhbs95","name":"BC Cancer Foundation","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cancer","B-Cell Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n5j5pmbhrm","title":"Supplementary Table. Components, mechanisms of action, and clinical indications of FDA-approved ADCs","description":"Indications obtained from FDA package inserts. \nADC = Antibody-drug conjugate; AML = acute myelogenous leukemia; ALCL = anaplastic large cell lymphoma; ALL = acute lymphoblastic leukemia; DLBCL = diffuse large B cell lymphoma; DM4 = maytansine; FDA = Food and Drug Administration; FRα = folate receptor α; HR = hormone receptor; HER2 = human epidermal growth factor receptor 2; IgG = immunoglobulin; mAb = monoclonal antibody; MF = mycosis fungoides; MMAE = monomethyl auristatin E; NSCLC = non-small-cell lung cancer; PD-(L)1 = programmed cell death protein (Ligand) 1; TF = tissue factor; TROP-2 = trophoblastic antigen 2","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-03-15T14:30:38.515Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n5j5pmbhrm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n5j5pmbhrm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Gronbeck","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dermatology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nmxgxsvbk5","title":"Clinical Efficacy and Molecular Biomarkers in a Phase II Study of Tucidinostat Plus R-CHOP in Elderly Patients with Newly Diagnosed Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma","description":"Elderly patients with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) present with poor clinical outcome and intolerance to intensive chemotherapy. Histone deacetylase inhibitors (HDACIs) show anti-lymphoma activities and can be applied to treat DLBCL. We conducted a phase II study to to evaluate efficacy and safety of oral HDACI tucidinostat (formerly known as chidamide) plus R-CHOP (CR-CHOP) in elderly patients with newly diagnosed DLBCL (International Prognostic Index ≥ 2).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-19T15:07:28.938Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nmxgxsvbk5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nmxgxsvbk5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genome Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j7w83893f5","title":"Flow Cytometry of Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (Symphony cohort, 1 of 3)","description":"This dataset is the 1st of 3 parts and contains 23-color flow cytometry data from 133 diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLB) and 15 reactive lymph node (RLN) diagnostic biopsy samples. Each sample was stained with a 22-antibody T cell panel and data were acquired on a BD FACS Symphony instrument. The samples were acquired across 5 separate batch runs (#1-5). Each batch run also included an aliquot from a pool of 13 different RLN samples to serve as an internal staining/acquisition batch control. The file naming format is \"SymT2_DLB_A1234\" where \"Sym\" = Symphony, \"T\" = T cell panel, \"2\" = batch #2 (or 1, 3, 4, or 5), \"DLB\" = diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (or \"RLN\" = reactive lymph node), and \"A1234\" = unique sample identifier (or \"pooled\" = a pool of like samples).\n\nDiffuse large B-cell lymphoma is a common malignancy of mature B cells with heterogeneous outcomes. Prior studies have defined poor prognosis subtypes by features such as cell-of-origin; however, the role of the immune microenvironment in defining clinical outcomes is less clear. Here we used highly dimensional mass and flow cytometry with up to 40 and 22 antibody markers, respectively, to derive a single cell-resolved map of the tumor ecosystem encompassing both B- and T-cell compartments, along with reactive lymph node controls. Unlike our recent findings in follicular lymphoma (FL), B-cell phenotypes were largely unique to each patient and intratumoral diversity was neither prominent nor correlated with clinical outcome. Among tumor infiltrating T-cells, multidimensional analysis yielded 23-25 distinct clusters representing the full spectrum of activation/maturation states including terminally differentiated subsets which identified patients with inferior clinical outcome amongst a discovery cohort of 123 patients. Importantly, this observation was validated in an independent cohort of 151 patients using a single marker of terminal differentiation, CD57, in combination with routine CD4/CD8 T cell subsetting markers thus simplifying translation to routine clinical practice. The significance of CD8+ CD57+ T-cells was independent of cell-of-origin classification, suggesting this immune response feature is relevant across DLBCL subtypes. T-cell profiling can thus provide insight into potential tumor/immune cell interactions and may inform selection of immune-directed therapies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-17T14:21:33.997Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j7w83893f5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j7w83893f5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Weng","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03gqhbs95","name":"BC Cancer Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/04m0xav37","name":"Leukemia and Lymphoma Society","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cancer","B-Cell Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3r5bchvwx","title":"Flow Cytometry of Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (Symphony cohort, 3 of 3)","description":"This dataset is the 3rd of 3 parts and contains 23-color flow cytometry data from 133 diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLB) and 15 reactive lymph node (RLN) diagnostic biopsy samples. Each sample was stained with a 22-antibody T cell panel and data were acquired on a BD FACS Symphony instrument. The samples were acquired across 5 separate batch runs (#1-5). Each batch run also included an aliquot from a pool of 13 different RLN samples to serve as an internal staining/acquisition batch control. The file naming format is \"SymT2_DLB_A1234\" where \"Sym\" = Symphony, \"T\" = T cell panel, \"2\" = batch #2 (or 1, 3, 4, or 5), \"DLB\" = diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (or \"RLN\" = reactive lymph node), and \"A1234\" = unique sample identifier (or \"pooled\" = a pool of like samples).\n\nDiffuse large B-cell lymphoma is a common malignancy of mature B cells with heterogeneous outcomes. Prior studies have defined poor prognosis subtypes by features such as cell-of-origin; however, the role of the immune microenvironment in defining clinical outcomes is less clear. Here we used highly dimensional mass and flow cytometry with up to 40 and 22 antibody markers, respectively, to derive a single cell-resolved map of the tumor ecosystem encompassing both B- and T-cell compartments, along with reactive lymph node controls. Unlike our recent findings in follicular lymphoma (FL), B-cell phenotypes were largely unique to each patient and intratumoral diversity was neither prominent nor correlated with clinical outcome. Among tumor infiltrating T-cells, multidimensional analysis yielded 23-25 distinct clusters representing the full spectrum of activation/maturation states including terminally differentiated subsets which identified patients with inferior clinical outcome amongst a discovery cohort of 123 patients. Importantly, this observation was validated in an independent cohort of 151 patients using a single marker of terminal differentiation, CD57, in combination with routine CD4/CD8 T cell subsetting markers thus simplifying translation to routine clinical practice. The significance of CD8+ CD57+ T-cells was independent of cell-of-origin classification, suggesting this immune response feature is relevant across DLBCL subtypes. T-cell profiling can thus provide insight into potential tumor/immune cell interactions and may inform selection of immune-directed therapies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-17T14:21:53.813Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3r5bchvwx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3r5bchvwx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Weng","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03gqhbs95","name":"BC Cancer Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/04m0xav37","name":"Leukemia and Lymphoma Society","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cancer","B-Cell Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fj5r82gfvg","title":"Flow Cytometry of Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (Symphony cohort, 2 of 3)","description":"This dataset is the 2nd of 3 parts and contains 23-color flow cytometry data from 133 diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLB) and 15 reactive lymph node (RLN) diagnostic biopsy samples. Each sample was stained with a 22-antibody T cell panel and data were acquired on a BD FACS Symphony instrument. The samples were acquired across 5 separate batch runs (#1-5). Each batch run also included an aliquot from a pool of 13 different RLN samples to serve as an internal staining/acquisition batch control. The file naming format is \"SymT2_DLB_A1234\" where \"Sym\" = Symphony, \"T\" = T cell panel, \"2\" = batch #2 (or 1, 3, 4, or 5), \"DLB\" = diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (or \"RLN\" = reactive lymph node), and \"A1234\" = unique sample identifier (or \"pooled\" = a pool of like samples).\n\nDiffuse large B-cell lymphoma is a common malignancy of mature B cells with heterogeneous outcomes. Prior studies have defined poor prognosis subtypes by features such as cell-of-origin; however, the role of the immune microenvironment in defining clinical outcomes is less clear. Here we used highly dimensional mass and flow cytometry with up to 40 and 22 antibody markers, respectively, to derive a single cell-resolved map of the tumor ecosystem encompassing both B- and T-cell compartments, along with reactive lymph node controls. Unlike our recent findings in follicular lymphoma (FL), B-cell phenotypes were largely unique to each patient and intratumoral diversity was neither prominent nor correlated with clinical outcome. Among tumor infiltrating T-cells, multidimensional analysis yielded 23-25 distinct clusters representing the full spectrum of activation/maturation states including terminally differentiated subsets which identified patients with inferior clinical outcome amongst a discovery cohort of 123 patients. Importantly, this observation was validated in an independent cohort of 151 patients using a single marker of terminal differentiation, CD57, in combination with routine CD4/CD8 T cell subsetting markers thus simplifying translation to routine clinical practice. The significance of CD8+ CD57+ T-cells was independent of cell-of-origin classification, suggesting this immune response feature is relevant across DLBCL subtypes. T-cell profiling can thus provide insight into potential tumor/immune cell interactions and may inform selection of immune-directed therapies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-17T14:21:38.745Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fj5r82gfvg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fj5r82gfvg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Weng","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03gqhbs95","name":"BC Cancer Foundation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/04m0xav37","name":"Leukemia and Lymphoma Society","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cancer","B-Cell Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":138718,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":173,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":138718,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":137972,"restricted":70,"embargoed":676},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2286},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1363},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":702},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":471},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":413},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":391},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":313},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136070},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1902},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":140},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":42},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":887},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":827},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":522},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":363}]}}